---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Lab - Steve Wang"
  description="Smaller automation from Steve Wang's purchasing and sales reporting work — data pipelines, lapsed-customer reports, promotion impact reports, and spreadsheet extraction scripts."
  current="lab"
>
  <main>
    <section class="page-title section-wrap">
      <h1>Lab</h1>
      <p>
        Smaller pieces of automation from day-to-day purchasing and sales
        reporting work — not flagship projects. These run privately on
        internal data, so they are described here rather than hosted.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section-wrap project-list">
      <article class="detail-card" id="sales-data-pipeline">
        <div class="detail-header">
          <div>
            <span class="project-meta">Data pipeline</span>
            <h2>Weekly Sales Data Pipeline</h2>
          </div>
          <span class="status-label">Private · in weekly use</span>
        </div>
        <p>
          A Python importer that loads weekly Excel sales exports into a local
          SQLite database — header validation, per-file batch tracking, and
          duplicate-import resolution (replace / skip / abort) — with a flat
          CSV export for BI tools.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reporting needed a queryable history of weekly exports on a
          locked-down work machine; SQLite gives a zero-install local
          warehouse with no admin rights required.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card" id="lapsed-customer-reports">
        <div class="detail-header">
          <div>
            <span class="project-meta">Sales analysis</span>
            <h2>Lapsed-Customer Reports</h2>
          </div>
          <span class="status-label">Private · on request</span>
        </div>
        <p>
          Finds customers who stopped ordering given items — quantities are
          netted per invoice so fully returned orders don't count as
          purchases, with per-item or grouped output and "weeks since last
          order" thresholds to separate lapsed from active.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sales teams kept asking "who used to buy this and stopped?"; this
          turns a raw sales export into a formatted chase list in seconds
          instead of manual pivot work.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card" id="post-promotion-report">
        <div class="detail-header">
          <div>
            <span class="project-meta">Reporting automation</span>
            <h2>Post-Promotion Impact Report</h2>
          </div>
          <span class="status-label">Private · monthly</span>
        </div>
        <p>
          Cross-references monthly promotion item lists against the shared
          SQLite sales database to report which customers actually bought the
          promoted items, delivered as a formatted Excel workbook.
        </p>
        <p>
          Monthly promotions needed a follow-up answer to "did it move
          product, and with whom?" without re-opening a stack of weekly
          spreadsheets.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card" id="shipment-extractor">
        <div class="detail-header">
          <div>
            <span class="project-meta">Data extraction</span>
            <h2>Messy-Spreadsheet Shipment Extractor</h2>
          </div>
          <span class="status-label">Private · on request</span>
        </div>
        <p>
          Parses supplier stock workbooks that were formatted for humans —
          regex detection of shipment dates and quantities across
          inconsistent sheets and merged headers — into one clean
          incoming-shipments table.
        </p>
        <p>
          Supplier files arrive in a different layout every time; this
          replaces re-reading a dozen sheets by eye with one CSV.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
